WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:04.520
If you follow my advice
to eat a lot of greens daily

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.240
—and you should!—

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.160
just make sure to vary the types of greens
to avoid getting too many oxalates.

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.467
Watch the video for details.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:20.497
"Kidney Stones and Oxalates in
Spinach, Chard, and Beet Greens"

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.120
Kidney stones affect as many
as 1 in 10 people in their lifetime

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:28.400
and can cause excruciating pain.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:31.960
Makes me cross my legs
just thinking about them.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.720
Oxalate stones are
the most common type,

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.320
forming when the oxalate
concentration in your urine gets

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:43.280
so high it basically crystalizes
out a solution, like rock candy.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.600
Some foods, like spinach,
have lots of oxalates in them.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.480
Should we try to reduce our intake
of oxalates to lower our risk?

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:56.000
It turns out that people who
do get stones don't seem to eat

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.920
any more oxalates on average
than people who don't get stones.

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.840
It may be less what you eat
and more what you absorb.

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:08.800
People who are predisposed to
kidney stones just appear to be born

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.160
with a higher intestinal
oxalate absorption.

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:14.640
Their guts just really suck it up.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.360
So-called "super absorbers"
assimilate up to 50% more oxalate

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.840
than non-stone formers.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:26.600
Overall, the impact of typical dietary
oxalate on the amount of oxalates

00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:30.000
that end up in the urine
appears to be small.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.720
Even a massive dose of dietary
oxalates typically only results

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:39.120
in a relatively mild increase in the
amount that makes it into your urine.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.560
A 25-fold increase in
oxalate consumption

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:47.480
doesn't even double the concentration
of oxalates flowing through your kidneys,

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.440
so it's really more determined
by genetics than diet.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.360
But still, until you get
your first stone,

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.520
how do you know if you're
a super absorber or not?

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:03.560
Is it safer to just generally avoid
higher oxalate fruits and veggies?

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.160
People who eat more
fruits and vegetables

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.960
may actually tend to get
fewer kidney stones.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.680
When researchers put it to
the test and removed produce

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.800
from people's diets, their kidney
stone risk instead went up.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:21.560
Removing fruits and veggies can make
your dietary oxalate intake go down,

00:02:21.560 --> 00:02:26.080
but your body produces its own
oxalate internally as a waste product

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.880
that you may have a more
difficult time getting rid of,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.720
without the alkalizing effects of fruits
and vegetables on your urine pH.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:39.320
This may help explain why those eating
plant-based get fewer kidney stones,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:42.840
but it also may be due to their
cutting animal protein intake,

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.240
which can have an acid-forming
effect in the kidneys.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:50.800
We've known this for 40 years.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.000
Just a single can of daily tuna
fish can increase your risk

00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.040
of forming stones 250%, and even
just cutting back on animal protein

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:04.040
may help cut kidney
stone risk in half.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:08.760
Surely there's some level of oxalate
intake that could put people

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.960
at risk regardless.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.640
There have been a few rare cases
reported of people drinking green

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.800
juices and smoothies getting
oxalate kidney stones,

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.920
though most had
extenuating circumstances.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:24.360
This case describes a woman
whose kidneys shut down

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.960
after a 10-day juice cleanse, which
included two cups of spinach a day.

00:03:29.960 --> 00:03:33.320
Normally we might not expect a
cup or two of spinach to cause

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:37.840
such a violent reaction, but she
had two aggravating factors.

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.480
She had gastric bypass surgery, which
can increase oxalate absorption,

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:46.000
and a history of prolonged antibiotic use.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.920
There's actually a friendly bacteria
you want in your colon called oxalobacter

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:57.120
that eats oxalate for breakfast,
leaving even less for us to absorb,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:02.760
but it can get wiped out by long-term
broad spectrum antibiotic use.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.120
She still probably wouldn't have
run into a problem though,

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.560
if she had used something
other than spinach,

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:15.640
or beet greens or swiss chardâ€”
the trifecta of high-oxalate greens.

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:19.280
Kale has hundreds of times
less oxalates than spinach.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:25.440
She would have had to have juiced
in excess of 650 cups of kale a day

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.480
to get a comparable dose, so over those
10 days, more than 6,000 cups of kale.

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.640
But are the three high oxalate
greens only a problem for people

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:39.040
with extenuating circumstances
or who are otherwise at high risk?

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.880
And what if you cook the greens?

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.360
How much is too much?

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:48.921
I'll answer those questions, next.

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.680
The tragic case in which
a green smoothie cleanse

00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.360
shut down the kidneys of a woman,
who drank two cups of spinach a day

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.600
for just 10 days, is
complicated by the fact that

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:04.040
she had had a gastric bypass,
and was on prolonged antibiotics,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:07.520
which can both increase absorption
of the oxalates in spinach.

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:11.800
So can taking mega-doses of vitamin C.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.360
This guy went into kidney failure
juicing spinach and beet greens,

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.640
but he was also taking about
2,000 mg a day of vitamin C.

00:05:19.640 --> 00:05:23.400
Vitamin C is metabolized into
oxalate inside the body

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:26.840
and likely played a role
in his oxalate overload.

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:29.480
In both cases their juicing
alone was giving them

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:34.840
more than 1,200 mg of oxalate a day,
which is easy with spinach—

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:38.160
just two cups a day—but
practically impossible

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:40.640
with most other greens like kale,

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.600
requiring more than six
hundred cups a day.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:48.200
There is one case of apparent
dietary oxalate overload-

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:51.080
induced kidney failure uncomplicated

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:53.640
by surgery, antibiotics, or vitamin C.

00:05:53.640 --> 00:05:56.440
A man who had lost
about 80 pounds eating

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:58.440
a diet of greens, berries, and nuts,

00:05:58.440 --> 00:06:01.960
which evidently included
spinach six times a day.

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.240
Tragically, his kidney
function never recovered.

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:09.200
Remember that study purporting
to show a “massive” load

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.600
of dietary oxalate didn’t have
much of an effect on urine levels?

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:17.720
That study went up to
250 mg of oxalates a day.

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.440
That is massive if you were
talking about most greens.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:24.080
That would be 25 cups
of collard greens,

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:27.220
60 cups of mustard greens,
125 cups of kale,

00:06:27.220 --> 00:06:31.240
or 250 cups of bok choy at a time.

00:06:31.240 --> 00:06:35.840
But, that’s less than
one half cup of spinach.

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:37.840
Spinach really is an outlier.

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.480
Even though there’s
small amounts of oxalates

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.920
found throughout the food supply,
spinach alone may account

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:46.920
for 40 percent of oxalate
intake in the United States.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:49.880
The Harvard cohorts found
that men and older women

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:52.360
who ate spinach eight
or more times a month

00:06:52.360 --> 00:06:57.080
had about a 30 percent higher risk
of developing kidney stones.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.600
What if you cook it?

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:00.720
Oxalates are water soluble so,

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.040
for example, blanching collard
greens can reduce oxalate levels

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:09.880
by up to a third, so those 25 cups
at a time can then be 33!

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.800
For low-oxalate greens
it doesn’t matter,

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.160
cooked or not, since
they’re so low regardless.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:19.440
Steaming spinach reduces
oxalate levels 30 percent

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.800
and boiling cuts oxalate
levels more than half.

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:26.240
Boil the three high-oxalate
greens—spinach, beet greens,

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:28.960
and swiss chard—and 60
percent of the oxalates

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:31.600
are leached into the cooking water.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:34.600
They start out so high,
though, even cooked

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:38.760
would contain hundreds of times more
than low oxalate greens like kale.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:42.320
For high-oxalate greens it
doesn’t matter, cooked or not,

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:45.520
since they’re so high regardless.

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.920
The bottom line is that anyone
with a history of kidney stones,

00:07:48.920 --> 00:07:52.680
otherwise at high risk,
or who eats cups a day

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:55.640
should probably avoid the big three.

00:07:55.640 --> 00:07:58.760
This is especially important for
those who juice or blend their greens,

00:07:58.760 --> 00:08:01.480
as oxalates appear to be
absorbed more rapidly

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.640
in liquid than solid form.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:07.480
Another reason to give
preference to low-oxalate greens

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.440
is that they are less
stingy with their calcium.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.360
While less than a third of the
calcium in milks may be bioavailable,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:19.280
(whether from a cow or a plant),
most of the calcium in low-oxalate

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:21.160
vegetables is absorbed.

00:08:21.160 --> 00:08:25.480
The calcium bioavailability in
some greens is twice that of milk,

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.320
but the oxalates in spinach,
chard, and beet greens

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.560
bind to the calcium
preventing the absorption.

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:35.440
Other high-oxalate foods
that have been associated

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.840
with kidney problems at high enough

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:40.280
doses include chaga mushroom powder.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:45.280
Four to five teaspoons a day
and you can end up on dialysis;

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.160
four cups a day of rhubarb
is also not a good idea;

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:53.400
more than a cup a day
of almonds, or cashews.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:57.760
And then star fruit, which I
did a video in the past about:

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:02.200
a single dose of about a cup
and a quarter of star fruit juice,

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:05.040
or just four to six fruit.

00:09:05.040 --> 00:09:07.520
Excessive tea consumption
can be a problem,

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.360
especially instant tea,
which boosts urine oxalate

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.360
nearly four times
higher than brewed tea.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.600
Two cases of kidney damage
have been reported

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:20.200
both of which were attributed to
drinking a gallon of iced tea a day.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:27.544
Tea, like spinach, is super healthy;
just don’t overdo it.

